  <h2>Three plays, one clock</h2>
  <p class="lead">Workforce strategists sort the options into build, buy, and borrow. Most operations run the first two on repeat.</p>
  <div class="g3" style="margin-top:28px">
   <div class="card"><div class="blocknum">PLAY 01 · BUILD</div><h3>Hire and train</h3><p>Post the job, interview, onboard, train. It’s the right long-term answer, and the slowest one available. Direct hire typically runs 45 to 90 days to a productive crew, and that assumes the local market has the skills at all.</p></div>
   <div class="card"><div class="blocknum">PLAY 02 · BUY</div><h3>Call a temp agency</h3><p>Faster, at 14 to 35 days for most roles. But an agency hands you candidates to manage. Screening depth varies, and the crew is only as deep as the local pool. The onboarding, the scheduling, and the turnover all land on your supervisors.</p></div>
   <div class="card"><div class="blocknum">PLAY 03 · STRETCH</div><h3>Overtime on the core team</h3><p>The fastest play and the most expensive one to sustain. Overtime buys weeks, not months, and it spends your best people to do it. Burnout on the core team is one of the stakes the traditional playbook rarely prices in.</p></div>
